Rate Limiter

2.1
limting the number of times a user can request the server.

2.2
Types of algorithms to implement Rate Limiter :-
-> Token Bucket Algorithm
   in this , a bucket is present , and has refilling rate , so particular number of requests can be
   satisfied , and if overflow , then the request is rejected.
     refiller :- 3 tokens/second
     capacity :- 5 tokens

    pros :- easy to implement , allows a burst of traffic
    cons :- two parameters :- bucket size and token fill rate => tuning them is hard.

-> Leaky Bucket Algorithm
   requests made by user , is placed in the bucket.
   the bucket is leaky. 
   then request which will come out of bucket will be processed.
   the hole controls the flow of the processing
   and there is a capacity of bucket , if requests overflow , then the request will be dropped.

   pros :- suitable for a stable outflow rate .
   cons :- two parameters in the algorithm :- bucket size and rate at which request is processed.

-> Fixed Window Counter Algorithm
   in this , we are dividing timeline into fixed windows and assigning a counter for each window.
   once the counter reaches the threshold , new requests are dropped

  pros :- easy to understand
          spike in traffic at the edges of a window could causre more requests than the allowed 
          quota to go through (because it does't have strict limiting policies which is useful in
          some cases)

  cons :- spike in traffic at the edges of a window could causre more requests than the allowed 
          quota to go through

-> Sliding window log algorithm
   fixes the problem of sliding window counter algorithm.
   in this , the algorithm keeps track of timestamps , usually stored in cache.
   when a new request comes in , remove all the outdated timestamps.
   if the log size is the same or lower than the allowed count , a request is accepted,else rejected.

-> Sliding window counter algorithm
   (sliding window log algorithm + fixed window counter algorithm)
   we have fixed window , and then we are checking the percentage of current window and previous
   window. and then like count the number of requests , and if greater than the threshold, then
   the request is dropped.
   
